# Soak retention caps — CONFIG FRAGMENT. NOT APPLIED.
#
# This file is a proposal for the soak window, kept in the tree so the numbers and their
# reasoning travel with the code. Nothing here is read automatically, and no deployment
# step applies it: the running process keeps whatever its own environment says. Applying
# it is a deliberate act on the target's env file, followed by a restart — both of which
# belong to whoever owns that machine's window.
#
# ── Why these two lines ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both variables are 0 by default, and 0 means UNLIMITED, not "empty". Measured on the
# merged tree, N=200 client sends into ONE room, caps left at their defaults:
#
#     roomlog    1 → 201        (one entry per send, linear)
#     timeline   6 → 406        (linear)
#
# There is no third state: the guards read `if cap > 0`, so a default deployment keeps
# every event it has ever accepted, in two structures, forever. On a long quiet run that
# is the term that grows with TIME rather than with traffic, which is what a soak is
# measuring. Setting either variable to a positive number converts that term into a
# per-room constant — the growth does not get slower, it stops.
#
# Why 2000 and 5000:
#
#   * Every client read path in this project pages in tens of events, not thousands
#     (the probe shape used throughout this repo is ?limit=10). A served window of 2000
#     per room is two orders of magnitude above what a client asks for in one request,
#     so no ordinary backfill is ever cut short.
#   * timeline is what /sync and /messages serve, so it gets the SMALLER cap: it is the
#     structure that must not grow, and bounding it first is what makes RSS flat.
#   * roomlog is the deeper CRDT log behind it and gets 2.5× that. Catch-up, late
#     joiners and the drain path read the log, so keeping more than we serve means the
#     served window can be trimmed without also destroying what replication needs.
#   * The caps are PER ROOM. Multiplied by the handful of rooms a soak exercises, 2000
#     and 5000 are small numbers of entries next to any allocation worth measuring — the
#     point of the exercise is that the count stops depending on how long the run lasts.
#
# ── What a cap costs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A cap DELETES. Once a room exceeds it, the oldest events are dropped on the write path
# (oldest-first), and they are dropped for good: this is a retention decision, not a cache.
# Before applying it to anything whose history matters, read the number as "this server
# keeps the last N events per room and forgets the rest". On a test or soak target that is
# the intent. Elsewhere it is a policy call that belongs to the owner of the data.
#
# ── How to verify a run is actually capped, rather than assumed to be ──────────────────
#
# Reading RSS is not enough: a flat line can also mean the process died. Two cheap checks
# that need no restart and no code change:
#
#   1. print the caps IN EFFECT from the live process environment, and
#   2. confirm the served depth per room stops growing — the depth is visible from
#      outside through the messages endpoint, unlike the in-memory counters.
#
# Both are what scripts/soak-caps-report.sh does. Note what it deliberately cannot print:
# the sizes of the dedup set, the typing map's room keys and the abandoned-UIA set are
# in-process counters with no external read path. Their absence from the report is not a
# measurement of zero — see the script's own note.

MATRIX_HS_ROOMLOG_MAX_EVENTS=5000
MATRIX_HS_TIMELINE_MAX_EVENTS=2000
